--- negative control: tcpip-resolve-cname-not-followed ---
  facility: TCP/IP Services resolver -- the DNS reply parser of the shared resolver engine (tcpip_dns_parse_reply, src/vmstcpip/services/tcpip_resolve.h, vms-a9e7), the header the DCL TELNET/FTP/PING verbs ship and test_syssvc_bg_udp proves. A name not in TCPIP$HOST.DAT is resolved by an RFC 1035 query to the TCPIP$NAMESERVICE.DAT server over a BGn: UDP channel ($ASSIGN TCPIP$DEVICE:, IO$_SETMODE P2=IO$K_SOCK_UDP, IO$_ACCESS/WRITEVBLK/READVBLK); the parser follows CNAME records in the answer section to the A record.
  defect:   tcpip_dns_parse_reply() moves its owner target to the CNAME's canonical name with 'memcpy(target, next, sizeof(target));' before re-scanning the answers for the A record. The mutation drops that step, so a reply whose answer is CNAME -> A yields NODATA (the A record is owned by the canonical name, never by the alias). A direct A answer, NXDOMAIN, the wrong-ID decoy and the timeout paths are untouched, so only the CNAME assertion reddens. One statement neutralised.
